                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is a fun if flawed, little twin stick-ish shooter game. It clearly seems meant to operate that way but doesn't support controllers well. So it's using some awkward mouse and keyboard stuff that took getting used to.\n\nThe kind of janky and awkward art style gives it charm that grows on me. And the sound is decent but not like, amazing. There's lots of little reused clips and things that don't fit but are used as recurring jokes or lazy reuse like injury sounds or whatever.\n\nAt first, the twin stick shooter style felt a bit stiff but you really get into the groove and stocking ammo and explosives and managing your inventory is a nice little distraction as you work your way around the map taking out bad guys, tanks, clearing camps, etc.\n\nBut, there ends up being a lot less than you think. When you first look at the map and see about 20 places to go to, you think \"Woah, there's a lot to get through here\" but some of the locations cover about those 3-5 of those pins, and have actually realtively little to do there. They introduce some mechanics like clearing bunkers and things, but you only do it like 4 times throughout the whole game.\n\nThere are some hidden secrets and locations but it's pretty tedious to get there by having to swim to the furthest corner of the map and back and investigate every inch of the map to get them. Still fun, but the end kind of suddenly sprung on me with a sudden \"this is the point of no return\" and it tears away some of the mechanics you've been relying on which ramps the difficulty up to an honestly unfair level.\n\nThis culminates in a mechanic that, the first time you see it, feels novel and fun. A point and shoot Virtua cop style boss fight. But the final one, is one of the most insanely annoying sequences and took me a solid 20/30 tries and took a bit of luck to do. It's really a shame that they decided to focus on this instead of the established gameplay that they had been doing. Cause that final point and shoot fight just soured me on ever touching this again.\n\nTLDR: Fun, shows promise, but it is kinda cheap and not that polished. On to the sequel!",
